    "result_text": "The build is green \u2014 `Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0` / `BUILD SUCCESS`.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe E.164 telephone normalization in `OwnerRestControllerV1` already stripped formatting, applied the `+61` default country code, and enforced a generic 8\u201315 digit bound \u2014 but it never checked that the *national-number* length matched the specific country code. I added that per-country rule.\n\n**Changes to `src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/controller/v1/OwnerRestControllerV1.java`:**\n\n1. **`NATIONAL_NUMBER_LENGTHS` map** \u2014 the required national-digit count per calling code: `\"1\" \u2192 10`, `\"61\" \u2192 9`.\n\n2. **`validateNationalNumberLength(digits)`** \u2014 after the generic length check in `normalizeTelephone`, it strips the matched country code and throws `InvalidOwnerFieldsException(List.of(\"telephone\"))` (\u2192 HTTP 400) when the remaining national digits don't equal the expected count. Unknown country codes fall back to the generic bound. The two known prefixes are disjoint (`+1` numbers start with `1`, `+61` numbers start with `6`), so Map iteration order is irrelevant.\n\nThe reject case flows through the existing `InvalidOwnerFieldsException` \u2192 `ExceptionControllerAdvice` path, so `+61 123` (only 3 national digits) now returns 400, while the valid `04XXXXXXXX` payload (\u2192 `+61` + 9 national digits) still passes. The best-effort `toE164OrNull` duplicate-comparison helper simply treats now-invalid legacy values as non-matching, which doesn't affect any test.",
